**user**:
> Is this grammar right? "The contractor must perform the following tasks related to the preparation, organisation and realization of the conference during the implementation of the contract: ...". Shouldn't it be either organization or realisation?
**assistant**:
> The grammar is fine. But the spelling style is mixed:
> 
> - **US English:** “preparation, organization, and realization”
> - **British English (common):** “preparation, organisation, and realisation”
> 
> That said, **-ize** is also accepted in some British styles (Oxford spelling), so “organisation and realization” is not strictly wrong—but it is stylistically inconsistent in most contexts.
